{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VpByZDxUVFDwPcG5psUAwn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: backwards-compatibility-highest-priority\n3\tdescription: Never break existing clients — this is the single highest priority constraint in the project, above clean code and new features\n4\ttype: feedback\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\tNot breaking existing clients is the HIGHEST PRIORITY constraint. Every code change — adding new protocol versions, refactoring shared code, or fixing bugs — MUST preserve full compatibility with ALL previously supported clients. This takes precedence over clean code, performance, and new features.\n8\t\n9\t**Why:** The project supports many protocol versions simultaneously (RubyDung, Classic 0.0.15a, Classic v7, Alpha v1-v6, Beta, Netty 1.7-1.21+, MCPE, Bedrock). Changes to shared code paths have repeatedly caused regressions in other versions (e.g. wrong MCPE flags, broken block formats, world serialization order differences between Classic versions).\n10\t\n11\t**How to apply:**\n12\t- If a shared method is used by multiple protocol versions, NEVER change its behavior globally. Add a version parameter and branch, or create a version-specific override.\n13\t- Before editing any shared code, trace ALL callers to verify which clients are affected.\n14\t- Guard new behavior behind version checks so only the targeted client type is affected.\n15\t- After any change to packet handling, test at least one client from each supported version range.\n16\t- When adding version thresholds (e.g. `if (version >= V27)`), verify the threshold is correct for ALL versions in the affected range.\n17\t- The user expects to be told about regressions proactively, not discover them during testing.\n18\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7dbb5388-45d2-4c3a-877c-bd7f0e781aa5","uuid":"c25803a2-37da-4215-9769-ee1c343d13be","timestamp":"2026-11-25T02:53:22.591Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-yingxizhao-dev-cs2520-projects/40c7aa4c-2adf-44c6-8b63-be3856052b7e/scratchpad/ccwork/6550418575/ws/.claude/memory/feedback_backwards_compat.md","content":"---\nname: backwards-compatibility-highest-priority\ndescription: Never break existing clients — this is the single highest priority constraint in the project, above clean code and new features\ntype: feedback\n---\n\nNot breaking existing clients is the HIGHEST PRIORITY constraint. Every code change — adding new protocol versions, refactoring shared code, or fixing bugs — MUST preserve full compatibility with ALL previously supported clients. This takes precedence over clean code, performance, and new features.\n\n**Why:** The project supports many protocol versions simultaneously (RubyDung, Classic 0.0.15a, Classic v7, Alpha v1-v6, Beta, Netty 1.7-1.21+, MCPE, Bedrock). Changes to shared code paths have repeatedly caused regressions in other versions (e.g. wrong MCPE flags, broken block formats, world serialization order differences between Classic versions).\n\n**How to apply:**\n- If a shared method is used by multiple protocol versions, NEVER change its behavior globally. Add a version parameter and branch, or create a version-specific override.\n- Before editing any shared code, trace ALL callers to verify which clients are affected.\n- Guard new behavior behind version checks so only the targeted client type is affected.\n- After any change to packet handling, test at least one client from each supported version range.\n- When adding version thresholds (e.g. `if (version >= V27)`), verify the threshold is correct for ALL versions in the affected range.\n- The user expects to be told about regressions proactively, not discover them during testing.\n","numLines":18,"startLine":1,"totalLines":18}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VpByZDxUVFDwPcG5psUAwn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
